  "subtitle": "A dedicated word processor whose editing lives on labeled keys — IN, OUT, PRINT, and an OOPS key that undoes your mistake in one physical press",
  "why_scout_cares": "The museum covers text entry and word processors (Canon Cat, Microwriter, Brother WP1, Sony Typecorder), but nothing with a dedicated, physically-labeled editing keyboard where text surgery is a committed press on a named key. The CPT 8100's OOPS undo key and IN/OUT/PRINT block-move keys collapse 'editing' into labeled physical actions — editing as a ritual of named keys rather than menus or commands — and its full-page green CRT foregrounds the monitor-vs-printed-page conflict of the pre-WYSIWYG office. Real, widely-shipped, and unlike every other text device in the collection.",
  "search_terms": ["CPT 8100", "CPT 8000 word processor", "CPT Corporation editing keyboard", "OOPS key", "dedicated word processor"],
